          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"tolstoy-war-and-peace-63c8aa43804f6006\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":2,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.93,\"analysis\":\"The remembered “je vous aime” presents as true Pierre’s love for Hélène, so its primary force is truth-conditional even if the avowal was emotionally mistaken, insincere, or retrospectively repudiated. Its occurrence during his courtship gives it some force as a relational move toward marriage, but the available passage does not show that the words alone explicitly undertake a commitment. Nothing indicates reflex-like expressive discharge rather than an avowal delivered in a charged courtship situation.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The principal uncertainty is the degree of performative force: the declaration participates in a courtship, but courtship context alone does not establish that this particular formula constituted a commitment.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"quoted_or_revoiced\",\"description\":\"The narration quotes an actual earlier declaration Pierre made to Hélène, embedded within a retrospective representation of the self-reproach he formerly felt about having said it.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Pierre recalls having reproached himself with painful shame for saying “je vous aime” during his courtship of Hélène. The passage contrasts that former declaration with his present love for Natasha, whose words and gestures he recalls without wishing to alter anything. This framing casts doubt on the earlier declaration’s authenticity or emotional adequacy, but not on its semantic mode as an avowal; falsity or self-deception remains compatible with high T. Its matrimonial setting supplies moderate, not decisive, support for P.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“слов сказанных им” and “зачем, зачем я сказал тогда ‘je vous aime?’” identify the phrase as words Pierre had actually spoken and later regretted saying.\",\"supports\":\"The classified event is a past utterance revoiced in retrospective thought, not a merely hypothetical declaration.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narrator places the earlier words “во время его сватовства с Элен” (“during his courtship of Hélène”).\",\"supports\":\"The declaration had ordinary love-avowal force and some potential relational or courtship function, supporting high T and moderate P.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Pierre formerly repeated the words “с болезненным стыдом” (“with painful shame”), asking why he had said them.\",\"supports\":\"The earlier declaration is retrospectively repudiated or regarded as inappropriate; this bears on sincerity and context rather than changing its avowal mode.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration contrasts the Hélène episode with Pierre’s present state, in which he lovingly repeats every word exchanged with Natasha and wants neither to subtract nor add anything.\",\"supports\":\"The contrast suggests that the earlier declaration lacked the emotional congruence Pierre now experiences, while providing no evidence that it was an expressive/reflexive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is marked as an affirmative present-tense explicit love formula, with exact match “je vous aime.”\",\"supports\":\"The grammatical form directly presents a current loving state, supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the declaration’s avowal force, while P records its limited courtship-related undertaking pressure. Retrospective shame and possible emotional falsity are contextual qualifications, and there is no independent evidence for E or residual core force requiring O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The French phrase is embedded in Russian narration, but code-switching does not alter its ordinary love-declaration force.\"}"
